        "# 1. Problem framing and foundations — Required Text Cell\n",
        "\n",
        "One observation in this dataset is a single **route-day**: one bus/minibus route operated on one\n",
        "calendar day, described by scheduling, weather, road, sensor and operational fields. Candidate\n",
        "features include route, district, day type, weather, road condition, vehicle type, delay,\n",
        "speed, traffic index, occupancy and engine/brake/vibration sensor readings. The continuous\n",
        "target is **passenger_demand** (trips taken that day), and the binary target is **high_risk**\n",
        "(converted to 0/1), meaning the route-day is flagged as unusually unsafe. Predicting demand and\n",
        "clustering route patterns are unsupervised/regression tasks; predicting high_risk is a\n",
        "supervised classification task; dimensionality reduction (PCA/SVD/MDS) summarizes sensor\n",
        "patterns; the MLP and CNN sections are deep-learning tasks (tabular risk prediction and\n",
        "automobile/truck image classification). Classical programming applies hand-written rules to\n",
        "data, while machine learning learns the mapping from historical examples. Classical ML uses\n",
        "engineered tabular features with relatively simple models, while deep learning learns\n",
        "hierarchical representations directly from raw or lightly processed data (images, in Section 8).\n",
        "These predictions could support fleet scheduling, maintenance prioritisation and driver-safety\n",
        "interventions."
      ]
