      "source": [
        "# 3. Continuous demand prediction and regularization — Required Text Cell\n",
        "\n",
        "`passenger_demand` is a **regression** target because it is a numeric count that can take many\n",
        "ordered values, so the goal is to predict a real number rather than a class label. **OLS**\n",
        "minimises squared residuals; under a Gaussian-error assumption this is exactly the\n",
        "maximum-likelihood estimate, since minimising squared error is equivalent to maximising the\n",
        "likelihood of normally distributed noise around the true mean. **PLS** builds latent components\n",
        "that maximise covariance with the target (unlike PCA, which ignores the target), which helps\n",
        "when predictors are correlated. **Lasso** adds an L1 penalty on the coefficients, shrinking\n",
        "weak ones toward zero and setting some exactly to zero, giving automatic feature selection and\n",
        "a simpler, more stable model when many predictors are noisy or collinear. Comparing training and\n",
        "validation MAE/RMSE/R² reveals over/underfitting: a much lower training error than validation\n",
        "error signals overfitting (the model memorised training noise), while high error on both signals\n",
        "underfitting (the model is too simple). The test set is reserved and only touched once at the end\n",
        "because repeatedly checking it during tuning would let information about the test data leak into\n",
        "model-selection decisions, making the final performance estimate optimistically biased."
      ]
    },
