        "# 4. Binary risk classification, probability and error costs — Required Text Cell\n",
        "\n",
        "**Logistic regression** models the log-odds of `high_risk` as a linear function of the features\n",
        "and is fit by maximum likelihood, producing a calibrated-ish probability. **KNN** classifies a\n",
        "point by the majority label among its k nearest neighbours in feature space; because distance is\n",
        "scale-sensitive, unscaled features (e.g. `engine_temp_c` vs `occupancy_rate`) would dominate the\n",
        "distance calculation, so scaling is essential. A **decision tree** recursively splits on the\n",
        "feature/threshold that most reduces impurity, giving an interpretable, rule-like model. An\n",
        "**SVM** finds the maximum-margin separating hyperplane, and kernels (like RBF) implicitly map\n",
        "features into a higher-dimensional space to separate classes that are not linearly separable in\n",
        "the original space. A **Gaussian Naive Bayes** baseline applies Bayes' theorem: it combines a\n",
        "prior belief about how common high-risk days are with the likelihood of observing the given\n",
        "sensor/operational readings under each class to obtain a posterior probability of high risk.\n",
        "Probability estimation (a continuous score) is distinct from thresholding (converting that score\n",
        "into a 0/1 decision) — the choice of threshold, not the model, determines the decision. Because\n",
        "a missed high-risk route-day (false negative) can lead to an accident, it is far costlier than a\n",
        "false alarm (false positive), which only wastes an inspection. Lowering the decision threshold\n",
        "below 0.50 will predict \"high risk\" more often, increasing recall (fewer missed positives) at the\n",
        "cost of precision (more false alarms)."
      ]
